{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. Raymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states during the 12th century.\n\n**Early Life and Regency**\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His father Raymond II was killed by the Assassins in 1152. Since Raymond III was still a child his mother Hodierna of Jerusalem served as regent. Hodierna was the sister of Queen Melisende of Jerusalem and used her connections to secure her son's position. Raymond III was sent to the court of his cousin King Baldwin III of Jerusalem for his education. He was knighted by Baldwin III in 1161 and began to take on more responsibilities in Tripoli.\n\n**Political Career and Conflicts**\nRaymond III became fully independent in 1164 when he took control of Tripoli. His rule was marked by conflicts with Nur ad-Din the Zengid ruler of Damascus. In 1164 Raymond was captured at the Battle of Harim and spent almost a decade in prison. His mother Hodierna died in 1164 and his cousin Amalric I of Jerusalem became regent for Tripoli until Raymonds release in 1172. Upon his return Raymond III worked to strengthen Tripoli's defenses and alliances. He married Eschiva of Bures a niece of King Amalric I in 1174. Their marriage remained childless but Eschiva was a powerful figure in the county.\n\n**Later Years and Legacy**\nRaymond III became a key advisor to King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and later to Baldwin V. He served as regent for Baldwin V from 1185 to 1186. During this time he faced challenges from Guy of Lusignan who was backed by powerful nobles like Reynald of Châtillon. Raymond III opposed Guys claim to the throne and supported Sibylla and her son Baldwin V. After Baldwins death in 1186 Raymond initially refused to recognize Guy as king and allied with Saladin. He allowed Saladin to pass through his territory in 1187 which led to the disastrous Battle of Hattin where the Crusader army was destroyed. Raymond III died later that year. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and the wider Crusader states.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.3243790864944458}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.3991873562335968}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00013076403411105275}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.014716053381562233}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00010179955279454589}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -1.0132738680113107e-05}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.4040638208389282}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.17564749717712402}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.5735502529423684e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001934579631779343}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.2604601979255676}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.13905450701713562}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -1.0967194612021558e-05}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0007616956136189401}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0005634627304971218}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.1801649634435307e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00017128908075392246}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.000774798565544188}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00025245340657420456}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.42886292934417725}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.009803836233913898}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.0040289857424796e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.757642793469131e-05}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -3.576214658096433e-05}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -2.47952248173533e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.04213659092783928}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.014889185316860676}, {"token": " was", "logprob